From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: add the SM8350 camera clock controller

SM8350 has a camera clock controller at 0xad00000 that mainline does not
drive yet, so nothing in the camera subsystem (CSIPHY, CSID, IFE, CCI,
MCLK) can be clocked. Port it from the downstream camcc-lahaina driver.

The controller has six Lucid 5LPE PLLs and one Zonda PLL (PLL2, which
feeds the sensor MCLKs). Mainline has no separate 5LPE register layout for
either type: the Lucid 5LPE PLLs use the Lucid (Trion) offsets with the
Lucid 5LPE ops and are programmed with clk_lucid_5lpe_pll_configure(), and
PLL2 uses the Zonda offsets and ops. Downstream has two PLL2
configurations and picks the second one for "qcom,lahaina-camcc-v2",
which is what the stock devicetree of the Galaxy Z Flip3 uses. Only that
v2 configuration is carried here, so it applies to every SM8350 board;
v1 silicon is not handled.

Every RCG downstream parks on XO when disabled and runs with hardware
clock control; this maps to clk_rcg2_shared_ops and hw_clk_ctrl. The XO
and sleep sources keep clk_rcg2_ops. Downstream describes the GDSCs as
regulators in its devicetree; their offsets and flags come from there:
all retain their registers, BPS and IPE support hardware control, and
TITAN_TOP is modelled as the parent of the others as on the other camera
clock controllers. CAM_CC_GDSC_CLK is kept on at probe, like downstream.

The camera AHB clock is not an input: gcc-sm8350 keeps GCC_CAMERA_AHB_CLK
enabled itself and does not export it, as with the video and display
clock controllers. That leaves XO, active-only XO and sleep clock, the
same inputs as SM7150, so document the compatible in that schema and
allow the MMCX performance point it needs. Add the node to sm8350.dtsi
next to the video clock controller, on MMCX like it.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
